<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Qutlugh-Khanid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1222 CE – 1306 CE · capital Kerman</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty of Qara Khitai origin that ruled Kerman under Mongol overlordship and produced two notable women rulers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baraq-hajib">Baraq Hajib</a>, an officer of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qara-khitai">Qara Khitai</a> origin who had served the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a>, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> in 1222 during the chaos of the Mongol invasions. He submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and received the title Qutlugh Khan, which gave the dynasty its name.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Qutlugh-Khanids ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khans</a> and then of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, keeping the province prosperous through trade with the Gulf. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutlugh-terken-khatun">Qutlugh Terken Khatun</a>, widow of Qutb al-Din Muhammad, governed from 1257 to 1282 and was remembered for justice, irrigation works and building. Her daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padishah-khatun">Padishah Khatun</a>, wife successively of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhans</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaykhatu">Gaykhatu</a>, ruled Kerman from 1292 but was executed in 1295.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Family rivalries and Ilkhanid intervention weakened the dynasty. In 1306 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhan">Ilkhan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oljeitu">Öljeitü</a> ended its rule and placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerman">Kerman</a> under appointed governors.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1